  <h2>The conflict at the center of mold work</h2>
  <p>Mold jobs are priced by scope: how many square feet, how many rooms, how much containment, how much material comes out. Whoever defines that scope controls the price. In many mold jobs, the same company inspects, tests, reads the lab report, writes the scope and then does the removal.</p>
  <p>Two of the states we cover treat that as a conflict serious enough to regulate:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Texas</strong> licenses mold assessors and mold remediators separately through the Texas Department of Licensing and Regulation, and a licensee can't perform both the assessment and the remediation on the same project.</li>
    <li><strong>New York</strong> licenses mold assessors, remediation contractors and abatement workers under Article 32 of its Labor Law, which bars a licensee from performing both assessment and remediation on the same property, and bars anyone from owning an interest in both companies on that property.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Most other states have no mold licensing at all. That doesn't make the conflict go away. It just means you have to separate the roles yourself: one company, paid a flat fee with no stake in the size of the job, to assess; another to remediate.</p>

  <h2>What mold tests can and can't tell you</h2>
  <p>There are <strong>no federal standards for acceptable levels of mold</strong> indoors. The EPA's guidance is direct: if you can see or smell mold, you have a problem to fix, and sampling usually isn't necessary. That's worth knowing before you pay for a test.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>What it shows</th><th>Limits</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Air sample (spore trap)</strong></td><td>Spores in the air at that moment, by type, compared with an outdoor sample</td><td>A snapshot. Counts swing with weather, activity and time of day. Without an outdoor comparison, the number means little.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Surface sample (tape or swab)</strong></td><td>Whether a stain is mold, and roughly what kind</td><td>Says nothing about how far growth extends</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Wall cavity sample</strong></td><td>Air inside a closed wall</td><td>Useful for tracing hidden growth, easy to over-interpret</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>ERMI and similar DNA dust tests</strong></td><td>Mold DNA in settled dust</td><td>Developed as a research tool. The EPA has said it isn't validated for routine use in homes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Moisture readings</strong></td><td>Where materials are wet now</td><td>Often more useful than any spore test, because moisture is what grows mold</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Testing does have real uses: tracing a musty smell with no visible source, documenting a condition for an insurance or landlord dispute, and verifying that remediation worked. It's least useful as the opening move of a sales visit.</p>

  <h2>Scare tactics to recognize</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>"Toxic black mold."</strong> Color doesn't identify the species or the risk. The CDC and EPA treat all indoor mold growth the same way. See <a href="/guides/black-mold/">black mold</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>A lab report with red bars and no outdoor comparison.</strong> Indoor counts mean little until they're compared with outdoor air on the same day.</li>
    <li><strong>"Your whole house is contaminated"</strong> after a small leak in one room, without moisture readings to show where the water went.</li>
    <li><strong>"You need to leave tonight"</strong> for a small patch of growth in a basement. Who should take extra care is covered in <a href="/guides/mold-after-leak-safe-to-sleep/">safe to sleep</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Health diagnoses from a contractor.</strong> Mold can trigger allergies and asthma, and people with weakened immune systems face more risk. Medical questions belong with a doctor.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Treatments that don't remove mold</h2>
  <p>Mold, living or dead, can cause allergic reactions. That's why every credible guideline, from the EPA to the IICRC S520 standard, comes down to <strong>physical removal</strong> plus fixing the moisture. Be skeptical of any of these sold as the fix:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Upsell</th><th>The problem</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Fogging with a biocide</strong></td><td>Can kill surface mold but leaves it in place, and doesn't reach growth inside materials</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Ozone treatment</strong></td><td>The EPA says ozone at safe levels isn't effective against indoor pollutants, and higher levels irritate the lungs. It doesn't remove mold.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Painting over growth</strong> with "mold-killing" primer</td><td>The EPA advises against painting or caulking moldy surfaces. Sealers belong on cleaned framing after removal.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Air scrubbers alone</strong></td><td>HEPA air filtration is part of a remediation, not a replacement for removing the source</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Whole-house duct cleaning</strong></td><td>Sometimes needed, but only when growth or contamination in the ducts has been found, not as a standard add-on</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What a real remediation includes</h2>
  <p>Ask for a written <strong>scope of work</strong>, often called a protocol, before work starts. It should cover:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>The moisture source and how it's being fixed.</strong> Without this, the mold returns.</li>
    <li><strong>The affected area,</strong> with measurements and photos.</li>
    <li><strong>Containment</strong> sized to the job: plastic barriers and negative air pressure so spores don't spread to the rest of the house. The EPA's guidance scales precautions with the size of the area: under about 10 square feet is often manageable by a careful homeowner, while larger areas call for more protection.</li>
    <li><strong>Removal of damaged porous materials,</strong> like drywall, insulation and carpet, bagged inside the containment.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaning of what stays:</strong> HEPA vacuuming, then damp wiping, and sanding where growth is on framing.</li>
    <li><strong>Drying,</strong> confirmed with moisture readings. See <a href="/guides/moisture-meters-thermal-cameras-is-it-dry/">is it actually dry</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Verification</strong> against criteria written down in advance.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Clearance: what "done" should mean</h2>
  <p>The IICRC S520 standard describes three conditions. Condition 1 is normal background mold, the kind every house has. Condition 2 is settled spores from nearby growth. Condition 3 is actual growth. The goal of remediation is to return the area to <strong>Condition 1</strong>, not to zero spores, which isn't achievable anywhere.</p>
  <p>Verification usually means a visual inspection, moisture readings and sometimes sampling. It's most credible when done by someone who isn't being paid by the remediation company, against standards agreed before work started. If you hired an independent assessor, have them do the clearance too.</p>

  <h2>Pricing tells</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A price per square foot of "affected area"</strong> where the area was defined by the same company's test.</li>
    <li><strong>A scope that grows once walls are open.</strong> Sometimes that's legitimate, because hidden growth is common. Ask for photos and a written change order before more work is done.</li>
    <li><strong>A low price with no containment,</strong> which may spread spores into clean rooms.</li>
    <li><strong>Long-term "maintenance treatments"</strong> sold as a subscription.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The insurance angle</h2>
  <p>Many homeowners policies exclude mold or cap it with a <strong>sublimit</strong>, often a few thousand dollars and sometimes up to about $10,000, even when the water damage that caused it is covered. Read your policy's mold language before agreeing to a large scope, and see <a href="/guides/does-homeowners-insurance-cover-water-damage/">what insurance covers</a>. Mold caused by a recent, covered leak is usually handled as part of that water claim, so report it as early as possible.</p>

  <h2>Questions to ask before you hire</h2>
  <p><strong>For the assessor:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Do you also do remediation? If so, will you step aside on this project?</li>
    <li>Will you take an outdoor comparison sample and moisture readings?</li>
    <li>Will you write a protocol that any remediation company can bid on?</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>For the remediator:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Are your technicians trained to the IICRC S520 standard?</li>
    <li>How will you contain the area and protect the rest of the house?</li>
    <li>What exactly will you remove, and what will you clean and leave?</li>
    <li>What are the clearance criteria, and who checks them?</li>
    <li>Do you hold the license your state requires, if any?</li>
  </ul>

